Does food eaten when weak/fainting give more nutrition than food eaten
when not-hungry/satiated?
In other words, if I find a food ration and I'm not satiated, will I
stave off starvation longer if I  don't eat it until I'm hungry, or is
it better to simply store the food in my character's stomach where it
doesn't take up any inventory slots or encumbrance.

And on a similar note, why does food suddenly cease to weigh anything
once it's inside you? Couldn't a character's nutrition level have a
weight value? (to reflect food in digestive system/ fat reserves). As
you get hungrier, you get lighter. I realise this could cause a problem
with really heavy food items like dragon corpses, but those items give
far less nutrition than they weigh, so I assume the player just eats
the best bits and leaves most of the carcass to rot.

